<p>A Russian-flagged tanker, the Anatoly Kolodkin, arrived at Cuba’s port of Matanzas on March 30 carrying roughly 700,000 to 730,000 barrels, or about 100,000 metric tons, of crude oil after the United States allowed the delivery despite its broader effort to restrict fuel shipments to the island. President Trump said he had no objection to a country sending oil to Cuba “right now,” and the White House later said the waiver was granted for humanitarian reasons and did not represent a broader change in US policy toward Cuba. The shipment offers short-term relief to Cuba, which has gone months without an oil tanker and has faced blackouts and severe strain on an economy of about 10 million people. Several reports said the cargo could cover roughly two and a half weeks of supply and potentially stretch to about a month, buying Havana time rather than resolving its wider energy shortage. The decision also marked a notable tactical shift from recent US pressure on Cuba’s fuel imports, even as Washington insisted its overall Cuba policy remains unchanged and Russia said it had coordinated in advance with US counterparts over the vessel’s passage.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/KmyXWgoA7AkD6Kk1-XD0U0JYCPhIxIaSEnrNbIUGP3xNKRdCtv16r3BR8cRvE9EVOA42Fb8coCKflylPRgAJXeBXGmtQtfzvx6oULhM3mBbWMf-Y0tMxa9VcEdhwkrRn76t2TN4I6H6dY0lGVvk78Xw4cut4LR9-QQ1GQz3cIQzAp7r_Iq-Y8AqcHofF_SfGK7wq1KTdUJXDz7-sRu6QFA' alt='Satellite image of the port and oil terminal at the entrance to the Bay of Matanzas in northwestern Cuba.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>First cargo of 2026: Several reports described the Anatoly Kolodkin as Cuba’s first oil delivery of the year after months without a tanker.</li><li>Recent diversions: AllSides, citing the New York Times, said other Russian tankers loaded for Cuba were diverted the prior week under the blockade, underscoring how unusual this waiver was.</li><li>Trump’s framing: In remarks carried by Michael West, Trump minimized the move as “one boatload of oil,” suggesting the administration saw it as a narrow exception rather than a reopening of Cuba fuel trade.</li></ul><blockquote>If a country wants to send some oil into Cuba right now, I have no problem with that, whether it’s Russia or not - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Civilian-impact perspective: The Associated Press, cited by The Week, said the blockade was intended to force regime change but had “devastating effects” on civilians, leaving many desperate. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/us-russian-oil-tanker-reach-cuba'>The Week</a>)</li><li>Kremlin: Dmitry Peskov said Russia had a duty to support a friendly country and indicated Moscow would keep working on further supplies for Cuba despite U.S. restrictions. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/russia-glad-oil-cargo-reached-its-ally-cuba-amid-us-blockade/article70804256.ece'>The Hindu</a>, <a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/russian-oil-tanker-arrives-in-cuba-after-us-approval/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Legal-strategic perspective: Reporting cited by The Week said the administration had limited legal tools to stop the tanker and also avoided a potentially thorny confrontation with Russia near Florida. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/us-russian-oil-tanker-reach-cuba'>The Week</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/news-wrap-russian-oil-tanker-arrives-in-cuba'>News Wrap: Russian oil tanker arrives in Cuba</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-cuba-to-receive-first-oil-shipment-this-year-despite-us-blockade/'>Cuba to receive first oil shipment this year despite U.S. blockade</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/30/world/americas/russian-oil-tanker-cuba.html'>Why Did the U.S. Allow a Russian Oil Tanker Through Its Cuba Blockade?</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/russia-glad-